    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="1601" n="1539" sameAs="frus:frus1945v01/pg1539"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.C115 Paris/10–1145: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-10-11T12:00:00+01:00">October 11,
                            1945—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 11—10:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5971. For Wiesman<anchor n="21" xml:id="d381-f1"/> from Mulliken. Conversation
                    with British Government ILO (International Labor Office) representative reveals
                    that British favored extending ILO invitation to Bulgaria and Hungary and hence
                    are not likely to oppose presence of representatives. British oppose
                    constitutional action by ILO except that absolutely necessary. Canada taking
                    same constitutional position. [Mulliken.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d381-f1" n="21" xml:id="d381-f1-content">
                <p> Bernard Wiesman, Chief, International Labor Organizations Branch, Division of
                    International Labor, Social and Health Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
